      "text": "Let the unknown number be denoted by $x$. According to the given conditions, we can translate the statement into a mathematical inequality.\n\nThe difference when the product of 4 and the unknown number $x$ is subtracted from 16 is expressed as:\n\\[16 - 4x\\]\n\nThe statement \"the difference is greater than 10\" can be written as:\n\\[16 - 4x > 10\\]\n\nTo solve for $x$, we isolate $x$ on one side of the inequality. First, subtract 16 from both sides:\n\\[-4x > 10 - 16\\]\n\\[-4x > -6\\]\n\nNext, divide both sides by -4, which is the same as multiplying by the reciprocal of -4 (and reversing the inequality sign because we are dividing by a negative number):\n\\[x < \\frac{-6}{-4}\\]\n\\[x < \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nSince $x$ must be a positive integer, the possible values for $x$ are 1 and 2. Therefore, the positive integers that satisfy the given conditions are:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]\n\nTo verify, we substitute $x = 1$ and $x = 2$ into the original inequality:\n1. For $x = 1$:\n\\[16 - 4(1) = 16 - 4 = 12\\]\n\\[12 > 10\\]\nThis satisfies the inequality.\n\n2. For $x = 2$:\n\\[16 - 4(2) = 16 - 8 = 8\\]\n\\[8 > 10\\]\nThis does not satisfy the inequality.\n\nThus, the only positive integer that satisfies the given conditions is $x = 1$.",
